From 47be3e0c6c3d5ad03f9c5b85569c3920ac56e897 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: mesa-nvk-horizon <mesa-nvk-horizon@localhost>
Date: Mon, 7 Sep 2026 19:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 74/74] vulkan/wsi: Horizon, the layer's size comes from libnx,
 not the queue
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

mesa-nvk-horizon: Phase 6 item 5 (present modes and scaling)
Why: patch 0070 stopped re-reading NWindow::default_* on every surface
 query and latched it once instead, on the argument that the first ask
 always happens before this backend has registered anything. That is
 true of the first SURFACE in a process and of nothing else. A second
 surface over the same window latches after the first one's swapchains
 have been and gone, and `default_*` is then the queue's default buffer
 size, which is this process's previous registration arriving one
 connect late — the very lag 0070 exists to stop reporting.
Evidence: measured on a console 2026-09-07, sdmc:/horizon_gpu_tests/
 vk_wsi.log, with 0070 applied. vk_wsi/swapchain latched 1280x720 and
 passed 227/227. vk_wsi/suboptimal, which opens a surface of its own
 after it, logged
     wsi_horizon: the window already had dimensions 1280x720 set on it
     when the layer's size was first asked for; taking 640x360 as the
     layer
 — 0070's own warning, firing on exactly the case it was written to
 report. The case still passed 273/273, because everything downstream
 agreed with the wrong number; an application would have been told its
 layer had halved.
Upstream: no — libnx and NWindow exist nowhere else.

nwindowGetDimensions is the answer, and 0070 said it was not. What
changed between the two is 0070 itself: it made
wsi_horizon_release_window put NWindow::width back to the layer's size
every time this backend gives the window up. libnx returns `width` when
a producer has set it and `default_width` otherwise, so both of its
branches now mean the layer —

  - in a fresh process nothing has set the window, `width` is zero, and
    the answer is `default_*` from the first connect, which is the
    consumer's own with no producer echo in it yet;
  - afterwards `width` is what the last producer asked for, and the
    last producer to have this window was this backend, putting it
    back.

One field, written by us to the layer on release and read by us on
latch, instead of a field with two authors read at whatever moment a
surface happens to be created.

The warning goes with it, because the condition it fired on is now the
normal case. What replaces it is an INFO line, said only when the two
sources disagree, naming both — so a log still shows the lag when it is
there, and shows which of the two numbers this backend went with.

The case it cannot get right is a window somebody OUTSIDE this backend
configured: `width` is then that party's request and not the layer's,
and nothing available here tells them apart. An application that sets
its own window dimensions and then asks Vulkan what its surface is has
asked a question with two answers.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01YajZtX9CeYzC35x2t1QJJh
---
 src/vulkan/wsi/wsi_horizon.c | 70 +++++++++++++++++++++---------------
 1 file changed, 42 insertions(+), 28 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/vulkan/wsi/wsi_horizon.c b/src/vulkan/wsi/wsi_horizon.c
index c195fdb..a272f5f 100644
--- a/src/vulkan/wsi/wsi_horizon.c
+++ b/src/vulkan/wsi/wsi_horizon.c
@@ -354,15 +354,32 @@ wsi_horizon_platform(const struct wsi_device *wsi_device)
  * registration behind it happen — so the value latched here is the
  * consumer's own.
  *
- * `NWindow::width` is the tell. libnx leaves it zero until somebody
- * calls `nwindowSetDimensions`, so a non-zero width at latch time means
- * the window was already configured by something outside this backend,
- * and `default_*` may be that party's echo rather than the layer's.
- * That is worth one line in the log and is not worth failing over:
- * there is no other source. `nwindowGetDimensions` is not one —
- * it returns `nw->width` when it is set and `nw->default_width`
- * otherwise, so it is this field with a preference for the producer's
- * request in front of it.
+ * IT ASKS libnx, WHICH IS NOT WHAT THE OLD `wsi_horizon_get_extent`
+ * DID. `nwindowGetDimensions` returns `NWindow::width` when a producer
+ * has set it and `NWindow::default_width` otherwise, and in this
+ * backend both of those now mean the layer:
+ *
+ *   - in a fresh process nothing has set the window, so `width` is zero
+ *     and the answer is `default_*` from the first connect, which is
+ *     the consumer's own and has no producer echo in it yet;
+ *   - afterwards `width` is whatever the last producer asked for, and
+ *     `wsi_horizon_release_window` sets it back to the LAYER's size
+ *     every time this backend gives the window up. One field, written
+ *     by us to the layer on release and read by us on latch.
+ *
+ * `default_*` alone would be wrong for the second surface in a process,
+ * and MEASURED WRONG on 2026-09-07: `vk_wsi/suboptimal` opened after
+ * `vk_wsi/swapchain` had presented a 640x360 swapchain and read
+ * `default_*` as 640x360 while `NWindow::width` was the 1280x720 this
+ * backend had just put back — the queue's default buffer size lags a
+ * connect behind, and that lag is the whole defect this patch is about.
+ *
+ * The one case left is a window somebody OUTSIDE this backend
+ * configured, where `width` is that party's request and not the
+ * layer's. Nothing available here can tell that apart, so it is one
+ * line in the log rather than a failure: an application that sets its
+ * own window dimensions and then asks Vulkan what its surface is has
+ * asked a question with two answers.
  */
 static VkResult
 wsi_horizon_layer_extent(const struct wsi_device *wsi_device,
@@ -380,19 +397,13 @@ wsi_horizon_layer_extent(const struct wsi_device *wsi_device,
       return VK_SUCCESS;
    }
 
-   u32 width = win->default_width;
-   u32 height = win->default_height;
-
-   if (width == 0 || height == 0) {
-      /* Nothing has connected yet, so the consumer has said nothing.
-       * libnx's own accessor is the fallback, and on a window this
-       * backend has not touched it reads the same two fields. */
-      Result rc = nwindowGetDimensions(win, &width, &height);
-      if (R_FAILED(rc)) {
-         simple_mtx_unlock(&wsi->lock);
-         mesa_loge("wsi_horizon: nwindowGetDimensions failed: 0x%08x", rc);
-         return VK_ERROR_SURFACE_LOST_KHR;
-      }
+   u32 width = 0;
+   u32 height = 0;
+   Result rc = nwindowGetDimensions(win, &width, &height);
+   if (R_FAILED(rc)) {
+      simple_mtx_unlock(&wsi->lock);
+      mesa_loge("wsi_horizon: nwindowGetDimensions failed: 0x%08x", rc);
+      return VK_ERROR_SURFACE_LOST_KHR;
    }
 
    if (width == 0 || height == 0) {
@@ -400,12 +411,15 @@ wsi_horizon_layer_extent(const struct wsi_device *wsi_device,
       return VK_ERROR_SURFACE_LOST_KHR;
    }
 
-   if (win->width != 0 || win->height != 0) {
-      mesa_logw("wsi_horizon: the window already had dimensions %ux%u set on "
-                "it when the layer's size was first asked for; taking %ux%u "
-                "as the layer, which is the consumer's last word and may be "
-                "a previous producer's request",
-                win->width, win->height, width, height);
+   /* Said once, and only when the two sources disagree — which is when
+    * the queue's lag is visible and the reader might otherwise wonder
+    * which of the two numbers in the log this backend went with. */
+   if ((win->default_width != 0 || win->default_height != 0) &&
+       (win->default_width != width || win->default_height != height)) {
+      mesa_logi("wsi_horizon: taking %ux%u as the layer; the queue's default "
+                "buffer size says %ux%u, which is this process's previous "
+                "registration arriving a connect late",
+                width, height, win->default_width, win->default_height);
    }
 
    wsi->layer_window = win;
-- 
